Z||000000||fiero_nightmare||10-02-2001||08:26 AM||||what all cars have the exact same engine as a fiero GT? i am going out to all of the bone yards looking for a engine but first i need to know what i should look for. i need one with a distriberator (SP?) and i need one that i can put the fiero intake on.<P>what all cars and what years should i look for?<BR><P>------------------<BR>"pain is caused by pleasure"||||reg||1
Z||000001||Formula88||10-02-2001||10:32 AM||||Well, all of the iron head 2.8's in FWD cars should be close enough.  You want to find a 2.8 or maybe a 3.1 to find a car with a distributor.  They went to DIS ignition shortly after the 88 Fiero (some cars had it on the V6 before 88, I believe).<P>Chevy:<BR>Cavalier, Corsica, Lumina, Citation<P>Buick:<BR>Skylark, Somerset (?)<P>Olds Omega<P>Pontiac:<BR>Phoenix, 6000, Sunbird, Grand Prix<P>Not sure about the breakdown on years, but if it's a 2.8 or a 3.1 and has a distributor, you should be in business.  Remember, though, the Fiero heads are much higher flowing than any of the others you're going to find.  If you find a Citation X-11, it should have the same engine as the Fiero (the Fiero engine actually started off as the X-11 engine in 1980).<P>Or, you could just get a 3.4 out of a 93-95 F-body and swap the starter to the other side.  All the Fiero stuff bolts on and it's a bigger, more powerful engine.  Oh, and it has the good iron heads, too.<P>Note: You cannot use Aluminum heads with the Fiero accessories or intake.||||reg||4
Z||000002||terryk||10-02-2001||02:33 PM||||"If you find a Citation X-11, it should have the same engine as the Fiero (the Fiero engine actually started off as the X-11 engine in 1980)."<P>Actually, it was 1981. This would be the Z code engine, but it had the smaller crank journals but same heads and pistons, HVHP oil pump, etc.  <P>In 1985, they took the Z engine and added MPFI (making it the W code engine) and the larger journals. This engine would be exactly the same as the Fiero (minus exhaust, upper plenum, mass air-flow, etc.)<P>The X-11 Z code engine has much better upper end RPM than the Fiero (short runners), but lacks the lower RPM torque of the Fiero (long runners).<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||stimpy||10-02-2001||02:53 PM||||Wouldn't Cadillac Cimmaron's block work as well?||||reg||1
Z||000004||Ferrari_cdn||10-02-2001||08:29 PM||||"Olds Omega"<P>I drove that car for 2 years before this Fiero.  It IS a freaking slow engine!  Had to floor the pedal to get decent acceleration<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/ferrari_2.JPG">  <BR>'84 2m4, 4 speed, 106k miles (157k km)  AIM: CanadianFiero||||reg||13
Z||000005||terryk||10-02-2001||09:52 PM||||From 81 on, all X body cars had the Z engine option available although the X-11 was rated a bit higher due to the cold air induction hood (which really did work!). X-11's also had low ratio transmission although the 81 manual had a stretched 2nd gear.<P>Omega's, Skylarks, Phoenix, Citation. The tell-tale sign was the twin exhaust pipes or the Z in the 8th VIN position. <P>A nice engine for the time. X-11's are an interesting car with an interesting history.||||reg||1
